## Pattern Recognition Quick Reference

| If the problem involves... | ...Try this Pattern | Clue / Why? |
|---------------------------|---------------------|-------------|
| Contiguous subarrays or substrings | Sliding Window | You need to track a "window" of elements as it moves. |
| Sorted arrays or linked lists | Two Pointers | Often used to find pairs or triplets that meet a sum constraint. |
| Cycles or finding the middle | Fast & Slow Pointers | Pointers at different speeds will eventually meet if a cycle exists. |
| Overlapping time periods | Merge Intervals | Crucial for scheduling or finding intersections between ranges. |
| Top/Smallest K elements | Heaps / Top K | A Heap is the best way to track a specific number of "best" items. |
| Level-by-level traversal | Tree BFS | Uses a queue to process nodes one horizontal level at a time. |
| Dependencies (e.g., Course A before B) | Topological Sort | Finds a linear order for items that depend on each other. |
| Exhausting all possibilities | Backtracking | Used when you need to "try" paths and go back if they fail (e.g., Sudoku). |
| Optimal substructure / Overlapping subproblems | Dynamic Programming | Break problem into smaller subproblems, store results to avoid recomputation (e.g., Fibonacci, Knapsack). |